"""Explicit information, testing and causal toy models for batch 05."""
import math
from math_ops import op,OPS
def h(p):return -sum(x*math.log2(x) for x in [p,1-p] if x>0)
op('capacity','Outcomes Questions','Capacity','[Math.pow(p[0],p[1])]',lambda p:[p[0]**p[1]],'With {Outcomes} possible answers per question and {Questions} questions, what is the maximum number of distinct answer sequences?','Balanced questions can use the available answer sequences efficiently. Counting sequences is a capacity bound, not a guarantee that a particular puzzle achieves it.','grid')
op('binaryentropy','P','Bits','[-[p[0],1-p[0]].filter(x=>x>0).reduce((s,x)=>s+x*Math.log2(x),0)]',lambda p:[h(p[0])],'A binary toy answer is yes with probability {P}. What is its entropy in bits?','Balanced binary outcomes maximize the expected information of one answer. A rare answer is individually surprising but contributes little average probability mass.','grid')
op('bitsurprise','P','Bits','[-Math.log2(p[0])]',lambda p:[-math.log2(p[0])],'A specified toy outcome has probability {P}. How many bits of surprisal does -log2(P) assign it?','Surprisal measures one outcome; entropy averages over possible outcomes. Neither measures the semantic importance of the event.','grid')
op('passwordbits','Alphabet Length','Bits','[p[1]*Math.log2(p[0])]',lambda p:[p[1]*math.log2(p[0])],'A toy password draws {Length} characters independently and uniformly from an alphabet of {Alphabet}. What is its entropy in bits?','The calculation assumes uniform independent generation. A human pattern or reused password need not have the same entropy.','grid')
op('codeaverage','P1 P2 L1 L2 L3','MeanLength','[p[0]*p[2]+p[1]*p[3]+(1-p[0]-p[1])*p[4]]',lambda p:[p[0]*p[2]+p[1]*p[3]+(1-p[0]-p[1])*p[4]],'Three toy symbols have probabilities {P1},{P2},and the remainder; code lengths {L1},{L2},{L3}. What is the mean code length?','Frequent symbols have greater influence on average length. Code validity and the chosen symbol probabilities must be checked separately.','grid')
op('binarykl','P Q','KLBits','[p[0]*Math.log2(p[0]/p[1])+(1-p[0])*Math.log2((1-p[0])/(1-p[1]))]',lambda p:[p[0]*math.log2(p[0]/p[1])+(1-p[0])*math.log2((1-p[0])/(1-p[1]))],'Two toy Bernoulli distributions have probabilities P {P} and Q {Q}, both strictly between zero and one. What is KL(P||Q) in bits?','KL weights log probability ratios by the first distribution. Reversing its arguments changes those weights, so it need not be symmetric.','grid')
op('logloss','Y P','Loss','[-p[0]*Math.log(p[1])-(1-p[0])*Math.log(1-p[1])]',lambda p:[-p[0]*math.log(p[1])-(1-p[0])*math.log(1-p[1])],'A toy binary outcome is Y {Y}; predicted probability of class 1 is {P}. What is its binary log loss in nats?','Log loss grows sharply when a confident prediction assigns little probability to the observed class. Calibration requires repeated outcome checks.','grid')
op('squaredependence','ZeroShare','MutualBits Correlation','[-[p[0],1-p[0]].filter(x=>x>0).reduce((s,x)=>s+x*Math.log2(x),0),0]',lambda p:[h(p[0]),0],'Toy X is 0 with probability {ZeroShare}, otherwise equally likely -1 or +1. Y=X². What is the mutual information in bits?','Symmetry makes the linear correlation zero, while Y remains a deterministic function of X. Linear correlation does not capture every dependence.','grid')
op('correctable','Distance','Errors','[Math.floor((p[0]-1)/2)]',lambda p:[math.floor((p[0]-1)/2)],'A toy block code has minimum Hamming Distance {Distance}. How many arbitrary symbol errors can nearest-codeword decoding guarantee to correct?','Minimum distance supplies a worst-case correction bound. A percentage of covered image area is not automatically the same as this code-symbol error count.','grid')
def testinfo(p):
 positive=p[0]*p[1]+(1-p[0])*p[2];a=p[0]*p[1]/positive;b=p[0]*(1-p[1])/(1-positive)
 return [h(p[0])-positive*h(a)-(1-positive)*h(b)]
op('testinfo','Prior Sensitivity FalsePositive','Bits','[(()=>{const h=(x:number)=>-[x,1-x].filter(v=>v>0).reduce((s,v)=>s+v*Math.log2(v),0),q=p[0]*p[1]+(1-p[0])*p[2];return h(p[0])-q*h(p[0]*p[1]/q)-(1-q)*h(p[0]*(1-p[1])/(1-q));})()]',testinfo,'Toy prevalence is {Prior}; sensitivity {Sensitivity}, false-positive rate {FalsePositive}. What is prior binary entropy minus expected posterior entropy, in bits?','Average uncertainty reduction weighs positive and negative results by their frequencies. Information value and decision value are different quantities.','grid')
op('familyerror','Tests Alpha Corrected','FWER','[1-Math.pow(1-p[1]/(p[2]===1?p[0]:1),p[0])]',lambda p:[1-(1-p[1]/(p[0] if p[2]==1 else 1))**p[0]],'Run {Tests} independent true-null tests. Nominal Alpha is {Alpha}; Corrected {Corrected} means divide alpha by test count if 1, otherwise not. What is probability of any rejection?','This exact family calculation assumes independent null tests. Bonferroni’s union-bound guarantee can also be used without independence.','grid')
op('uniontests','Tests Alpha','Bound','[Math.min(1,p[0]*p[1])]',lambda p:[min(1,p[0]*p[1])],'There are {Tests} opportunities, each with false-alarm probability at most {Alpha}. What union-bound ceiling applies to any false alarm?','The bound needs no independence but can be loose. Repeated looks at shared data require their own sequential-testing model.','grid')
op('zscore','Difference SD N','Z','[p[0]*Math.sqrt(p[2])/p[1]]',lambda p:[p[0]*math.sqrt(p[2])/p[1]],'A toy mean difference is {Difference}, known observation SD {SD}, and sample size {N}. Using SE=SD/sqrt(N), what z score results?','A fixed small effect can have a large standardized test statistic at large N. Statistical separation does not establish practical importance.','grid')
def choose(n,k):return math.comb(int(n),int(k)) if 0<=k<=n else 0
op('hypergeom','Population Marked Draws AtLeast','Tail','[(()=>{const c=(n:number,k:number)=>{if(k<0||k>n)return 0;let v=1;for(let i=1;i<=k;i++)v=v*(n-i+1)/i;return v;};let sum=0;for(let k=p[3];k<=Math.min(p[1],p[2]);k++)sum+=c(p[1],k)*c(p[0]-p[1],p[2]-k);return sum/c(p[0],p[2]);})()]',lambda p:[sum(choose(p[1],k)*choose(p[0]-p[1],p[2]-k) for k in range(int(p[3]),int(min(p[1],p[2]))+1))/choose(p[0],p[2])],'Draw {Draws} without replacement from {Population} items, {Marked} marked. What is the probability of at least {AtLeast} marked draws?','Conditioning on fixed margins gives this hypergeometric reference distribution. It counts possible allocations under the stated randomization model.','grid')
op('chi3','A B C','Statistic','[(()=>{const e=(p[0]+p[1]+p[2])/3;return p.reduce((s,x)=>s+(x-e)*(x-e)/e,0);})()]',lambda p:[sum((x-sum(p)/3)**2/(sum(p)/3) for x in p)],'Three toy category counts are {A},{B},{C}. Under equal expected shares, what is the Pearson chi-square statistic?','Compare observed counts with expected counts using the same total. A statistic alone is not a p-value without a reference distribution and assumptions.','grid')
op('tailcount','OneTail Tails','Probability','[Math.min(1,p[0]*p[1])]',lambda p:[min(1,p[0]*p[1])],'Each of {Tails} disjoint symmetric tails has toy probability {OneTail}. What is their combined probability?','Adding the tails changes the rejection event. A one-sided direction should be chosen with the question, before inspecting the effect sign.','grid')
op('waldinterval','Estimate SE Z','Lower Upper','[p[0]-p[2]*p[1],p[0]+p[2]*p[1]]',lambda p:[p[0]-p[2]*p[1],p[0]+p[2]*p[1]],'A toy normal estimate is {Estimate} with known SE {SE}. What is the lower endpoint of estimate ±{Z} SE?','Effect magnitude and uncertainty both appear in the interval. A repeated-sampling interpretation depends on the stated estimator and model assumptions.','intervals')
op('mse','Bias Variance Noise','MSE','[p[0]*p[0]+p[1]+p[2]]',lambda p:[p[0]**2+p[1]+p[2]],'A toy squared-error decomposition has Bias {Bias}, estimation Variance {Variance}, and irreducible Noise variance {Noise}. What MSE results?','Bias squared, variance and noise contribute separately. Lower training error does not establish lower new-data error.','grid')
op('ridge','XY XX Penalty','Coefficient','[p[0]/(p[1]+p[2])]',lambda p:[p[0]/(p[1]+p[2])],'A centered one-predictor toy regression has sum XY {XY}, sum X² {XX}, and ridge Penalty {Penalty}. What coefficient results?','The penalty enlarges the denominator and shrinks the coefficient. Intercepts and feature scaling require separate specification.','grid')
op('capture','Tagged Sample Recaptured','Population','[p[0]*p[1]/p[2]]',lambda p:[p[0]*p[1]/p[2]],'A toy closed population has {Tagged} tagged members. A later random sample has size {Sample}, with {Recaptured} tagged. What simple capture-recapture estimate results?','The estimate matches the tagged fractions. Mixing, tag retention and equal capture chances are assumptions, not consequences of the formula.','grid')
op('line','Intercept Slope X','Prediction','[p[0]+p[1]*p[2]]',lambda p:[p[0]+p[1]*p[2]],'A toy fitted line has Intercept {Intercept}, Slope {Slope}; what does it predict at X {X}?','A line mechanically extrapolates beyond observed inputs. Whether its slope persists is an additional empirical assumption.','grid')
op('productrange','LowA HighA LowB HighB','Lower Upper','[p[0]*p[2],p[1]*p[3]]',lambda p:[p[0]*p[2],p[1]*p[3]],'Two positive toy factors range from {LowA} to {HighA}, and {LowB} to {HighB}. What is the smallest product allowed by these bounds?','Range propagation is not a probability interval. The endpoints describe allowed combinations under the stated positive-factor bounds.','grid')
op('confounded','Direct Common Corr Do','Slope TotalEffect','[p[3]===1?p[0]:p[0]+p[1]*p[2],p[0]]',lambda p:[p[0] if p[3]==1 else p[0]+p[1]*p[2],p[0]],'Standardized toy X and U have Corr {Corr}; Y=Direct {Direct}×X+Common {Common}×U+noise. Do {Do}=0 asks the observational slope, 1 the intervention derivative. What results?','The common cause can change the observational slope without changing the intervention derivative. The causal conclusion follows the specified structural equations.','graph')
op('collider','PX PY Selected XValue','YGiven','[p[2]===1&&p[3]===0?1:p[1]]',lambda p:[1 if p[2]==1 and p[3]==0 else p[1]],'Independent toy binary X,Y have probabilities {PX},{PY}. Selected {Selected}=1 keeps only X OR Y=1; otherwise all. Given X={XValue}, what is P(Y=1) in the retained set?','Selecting a common consequence can create a negative association between initially independent causes. Conditioning and intervening answer different questions.','graph')
op('mediation','Direct XM MY','Total Indirect','[p[0]+p[1]*p[2],p[1]*p[2]]',lambda p:[p[0]+p[1]*p[2],p[1]*p[2]],'A toy linear model has X→Y coefficient {Direct}, X→M coefficient {XM}, M→Y coefficient {MY}, with no interactions or confounding. What is the total X effect?','The total includes the direct path and the product along the mediator path. This decomposition needs the specified structural assumptions.','graph')
op('ite','Without With','Effect','[p[1]-p[0]]',lambda p:[p[1]-p[0]],'In a toy structural model the same unit has outcome {Without} without treatment and {With} with treatment. What individual effect is with minus without?','The two potential outcomes are defined in the model, although only one would be observed for a unit in an ordinary trial.','grid')
op('heteroeffect','Share EffectA EffectB','Average','[p[0]*p[1]+(1-p[0])*p[2]]',lambda p:[p[0]*p[1]+(1-p[0])*p[2]],'A toy population has share {Share} with effect {EffectA}, the rest with effect {EffectB}. What is its average treatment effect?','Averages can hide opposite effects across groups. Targeting requires an identified effect for the relevant population, not just a large average.','grid')
op('did','TBefore TAfter CBefore CAfter','Estimate','[(p[1]-p[0])-(p[3]-p[2])]',lambda p:[p[1]-p[0]-p[3]+p[2]],'Toy treated outcomes move {TBefore}→{TAfter}; controls move {CBefore}→{CAfter}. What difference-in-differences results?','The arithmetic removes the control change. Interpreting it causally requires parallel untreated trends and other design assumptions.','grid')
op('wald','Reduced FirstStage','Ratio','[p[0]/p[1]]',lambda p:[p[0]/p[1]],'A toy instrument changes mean outcome by {Reduced} and treatment uptake by {FirstStage}. What is the Wald ratio?','A small first stage amplifies the ratio. Exclusion, instrument validity and the target-effect assumptions cannot be inferred from this division.','graph')
op('rdjump','Left Right','Jump','[p[1]-p[0]]',lambda p:[p[1]-p[0]],'A toy threshold design has limiting outcome {Left} just below and {Right} just above the cutoff. What is the jump?','The local difference is a causal effect only under the relevant continuity and assignment assumptions. A large selected window can answer a different question.','grid')
op('ipweight','Propensity Treated','Weight','[p[1]===1?1/p[0]:1/(1-p[0])]',lambda p:[1/p[0] if p[1]==1 else 1/(1-p[0])],'A toy unit has treatment propensity {Propensity}. Treated {Treated}=1 uses inverse treatment probability; 0 uses inverse control probability. What weight results?','Rare observed assignments receive large weights. Weighting cannot recover comparisons with no overlap or fix unmeasured confounding by itself.','grid')
op('stdgap','MeanA MeanB SD','Difference','[(p[0]-p[1])/p[2]]',lambda p:[(p[0]-p[1])/p[2]],'Toy covariate means are {MeanA} and {MeanB}, with chosen reference SD {SD}. What is their standardized mean difference?','Balance diagnostics describe observed covariates on a common scale. Observed balance cannot demonstrate balance of every unmeasured cause.','grid')
op('harmonic','Out Back','Speed','[2*p[0]*p[1]/(p[0]+p[1])]',lambda p:[2*p[0]*p[1]/(p[0]+p[1])],'Travel equal distances at toy speeds {Out} out and {Back} back. What is total distance divided by total time?','Equal distances give a harmonic rather than arithmetic mean of speeds. The slower leg occupies more time.','grid')
op('riskchange','Baseline Treated','Reduction NNT Ratio','[p[0]-p[1],1/(p[0]-p[1]),p[1]/p[0]]',lambda p:[p[0]-p[1],1/(p[0]-p[1]),p[1]/p[0]],'A toy untreated risk is {Baseline}, treated risk {Treated}, with a nonzero difference. What is absolute risk reduction?','Absolute and relative changes use different scales. The reciprocal reduction is an average number-needed-to-treat under this specified causal-risk model.','grid')
op('spillover','Direct Spill TreatedNeighbors','Total','[p[0]+p[1]*p[2]]',lambda p:[p[0]+p[1]*p[2]],'A toy unit gets direct effect {Direct} plus Spill {Spill} per treated neighbor. With {TreatedNeighbors} treated neighbors, what total effect results?','With interference, exposure includes neighbors’ assignments. A single treatment indicator is insufficient to define every unit’s outcome.','graph')

OPS['confounded']['graph']='fork'
OPS['collider']['graph']='collider'
OPS['mediation']['graph']='chain'
OPS['wald']['graph']='instrument'
OPS['spillover']['graph']='spillover'

op('simpson','WeightA WeightB Rate1 Rate2 Advantage','Gap AggregateA AggregateB','[(p[0]-p[1])*(p[2]-p[3])+p[4],p[0]*p[2]+(1-p[0])*p[3]+p[4],p[1]*p[2]+(1-p[1])*p[3]]',lambda p:[(p[0]-p[1])*(p[2]-p[3])+p[4],p[0]*p[2]+(1-p[0])*p[3]+p[4],p[1]*p[2]+(1-p[1])*p[3]],'Two toy strata have B rates {Rate1},{Rate2}; A is better by {Advantage} in both. Stratum-1 shares are {WeightA} in A, {WeightB} in B. What is aggregate A minus B?','Each stratum has the same stipulated advantage, but different weights can reverse the aggregate comparison. Equalizing weights removes this composition channel.','grid')
op('selectedmean','Good Bad Share KeepGood KeepBad','Selected Full','[(p[0]*p[2]*p[3]+p[1]*(1-p[2])*p[4])/(p[2]*p[3]+(1-p[2])*p[4]),p[0]*p[2]+p[1]*(1-p[2])]',lambda p:[(p[0]*p[2]*p[3]+p[1]*(1-p[2])*p[4])/(p[2]*p[3]+(1-p[2])*p[4]),p[0]*p[2]+p[1]*(1-p[2])],'A toy population has Good outcome {Good} with share {Share}, otherwise Bad {Bad}. Retain fractions {KeepGood} and {KeepBad} respectively. What mean is observed among retained units?','Outcome-dependent inclusion changes the observed mixture. A large selected sample cannot by itself repair missing low-retention outcomes.','grid')
op('immortaltime','Deaths PersonTime Guaranteed People','Rate','[p[0]/(p[1]+p[2]*p[3])]',lambda p:[p[0]/(p[1]+p[2]*p[3])],'A toy analysis records {Deaths} events over {PersonTime} time units, then credits {Guaranteed} guaranteed event-free units for each of {People} people. What recorded event rate results?','Crediting guaranteed survival time enlarges the denominator without adding possible events. Time-zero alignment is essential to a causal survival comparison.','grid')
op('twosamplesd','P PerArm','SE','[Math.sqrt(2*p[0]*(1-p[0])/p[1])]',lambda p:[math.sqrt(2*p[0]*(1-p[0])/p[1])],'Two independent toy groups each contain {PerArm} observations with common binary-trait probability {P}. What is the SD of the difference in trait proportions?','Randomization balances traits in expectation, not exactly in every realized sample. Chance imbalance shrinks with sample size.','grid')
op('dsep','Pattern Conditioned','Active','[(()=>{if(!Number.isInteger(p[0])||p[0]<0||p[0]>2||![0,1].includes(p[1]))throw Error("Invalid graph choice");return p[0]===2?p[1]:1-p[1];})()]',lambda p:[p[1] if p[0]==2 else 1-p[1]],'A three-node path is Pattern {Pattern}: 0=fork, 1=chain, 2=collider. The middle node is conditioned on if Conditioned {Conditioned}=1. Is the path active (1) or blocked (0)?','Conditioning blocks a noncollider path but opens a collider path. This path test assumes no other paths or conditioned descendants.','graph')
OPS['dsep']['graph']='dsep';OPS['dsep']['binary']=True
op('attributable','Exposed RR','Fraction','[p[0]*(p[1]-1)/(1+p[0]*(p[1]-1))]',lambda p:[p[0]*(p[1]-1)/(1+p[0]*(p[1]-1))],'Under a toy homogeneous causal risk-ratio model, exposure prevalence is {Exposed} and RR {RR}. What population attributable fraction follows if exposure is removed?','The fraction requires causal and transport assumptions, not just an observed association. Removing an exposure may change other pathways absent from this toy model.','grid')
op('relativeevent','With Without','Ratio Attributable','[p[0]/p[1],1-p[1]/p[0]]',lambda p:[p[0]/p[1],1-p[1]/p[0]],'A toy event has probability {With} in one modeled world and {Without} in another. What is their probability ratio?','Comparing modeled worlds depends on their design and assumptions. A probability ratio is not the probability that one specific observed event was caused by the changed factor.','grid')
op('counterlinear','Slope ObservedX ObservedY NewX','Counterfactual Noise','[p[2]+p[0]*(p[3]-p[1]),p[2]-p[0]*p[1]]',lambda p:[p[2]+p[0]*(p[3]-p[1]),p[2]-p[0]*p[1]],'Toy Y=Slope {Slope}×X+U. Observe X={ObservedX},Y={ObservedY}; hold inferred U fixed and intervene X={NewX}. What counterfactual Y results?','Infer the disturbance from the observation, change the assigned cause, and reuse that disturbance. The answer depends on this specified structural model.','graph')
OPS['counterlinear']['graph']='counterfactual'
op('synthetic','Weight DonorA DonorB Actual','Gap Synthetic','[p[3]-(p[0]*p[1]+(1-p[0])*p[2]),p[0]*p[1]+(1-p[0])*p[2]]',lambda p:[p[3]-(p[0]*p[1]+(1-p[0])*p[2]),p[0]*p[1]+(1-p[0])*p[2]],'A toy synthetic control uses weight {Weight} on donor A {DonorA}, remainder on donor B {DonorB}; actual outcome {Actual}. What is actual minus synthetic?','The weighted counterfactual is model-based. Pre-period fit and donor suitability matter before treating this gap as a causal estimate.','grid')
op('evalue','RR','EValue','[p[0]+Math.sqrt(p[0]*(p[0]-1))]',lambda p:[p[0]+math.sqrt(p[0]*(p[0]-1))],'For a toy risk ratio RR {RR} at least 1, evaluate the sensitivity function RR+sqrt(RR×(RR-1)). What value results?','This risk-ratio sensitivity summary concerns unmeasured confounding under its assumptions. It does not address all biases or certify causation.','grid')
OPS['evalue']['reference']='https://content.sph.harvard.edu/wwwhsph/sites/603/2017/08/EValue_Preprint.pdf'
op('caliper','Gap Tolerance','Matched','[p[0]<=p[1]?1:0]',lambda p:[1 if p[0]<=p[1] else 0],'A toy pair has score gap {Gap}; accept a match when gap is at most Tolerance {Tolerance}. Is it retained (1) or rejected (0)?','A tighter caliper can improve closeness while excluding units. Retained pairs need not represent the original population.','grid')
OPS['caliper']['binary']=True
op('band','Age Width','Group','[Math.floor(p[0]/p[1])]',lambda p:[math.floor(p[0]/p[1])],'Coarsen toy Age {Age} into bands of positive Width {Width}, starting at zero. What zero-based band index is assigned?','Coarsening makes an exact match within a band possible but does not make all people in that band identical. Boundary choices affect membership.','grid')
op('balanceweight','Target Low High','HighWeight','[(p[0]-p[1])/(p[2]-p[1])]',lambda p:[(p[0]-p[1])/(p[2]-p[1])],'Mix toy values Low {Low} and High {High} to obtain Target mean {Target}. What weight on High is required?','Matching a mean can be solved algebraically when the target lies inside the support. Matching that moment does not guarantee every distributional or causal property.','grid')
op('oddsbias','Gamma','UpperProbability','[p[0]/(1+p[0])]',lambda p:[p[0]/(1+p[0])],'In a toy matched pair, hidden assignment odds can differ by factor Gamma {Gamma} at least 1. What upper assignment probability Gamma/(1+Gamma) follows?','This bounds assignment within the stated pair. A full sensitivity conclusion also depends on outcomes, the test and its assumptions.','grid')
op('policyvalue','Baseline Uplift Cost People','Increment','[(p[1]-p[2])*p[3]]',lambda p:[(p[1]-p[2])*p[3]],'A toy unit has baseline purchase chance {Baseline}, causal revenue lift {Uplift}, and treatment cost {Cost}. For {People} treated units, what incremental net value results?','Incremental value uses the change caused by treatment, not baseline purchase propensity. The model assumes the specified causal lift and common value units.','grid')
op('interaction','Base RiskRatio HighBase','LowDifference HighDifference','[p[0]*(p[1]-1),p[2]*(p[1]-1)]',lambda p:[p[0]*(p[1]-1),p[2]*(p[1]-1)],'Two toy groups have baseline risks {Base} and {HighBase}, sharing risk ratio {RiskRatio}. What is the first group’s treated-minus-baseline risk difference?','A common risk ratio can coexist with different absolute risk differences. Interaction is a property of both the model and the chosen effect scale.','grid')
op('feedback','Initial Amplification Steps','Level','[p[0]*Math.pow(p[1],p[2])]',lambda p:[p[0]*p[1]**p[2]],'A toy feedback process starts at {Initial}; each step multiplies the current level by {Amplification}. What level follows {Steps} steps?','A self-reinforcing measurement process can amplify its starting differences. The recurrence is assumed and does not separate true underlying need from observed activity.','timeline')
op('lagged','Direct Carry PreviousGap','ObservedGap','[p[0]+p[1]*p[2]]',lambda p:[p[0]+p[1]*p[2]],'A toy current outcome gap combines Direct {Direct} and carryover coefficient {Carry} times previous-exposure gap {PreviousGap}. What gap is observed?','A lag can contaminate a current-period contrast. Timing and washout assumptions matter before calling the observed gap a direct effect.','grid')
op('displacement','OwnLift Stolen','MeasuredGap NetLift','[p[0]+p[1],p[0]-p[1]]',lambda p:[p[0]+p[1],p[0]-p[1]],'A toy seller treatment adds OwnLift {OwnLift} to treated sales but takes Stolen {Stolen} from controls. What treated-minus-control gap is measured?','A control loss can enlarge the experimental contrast without creating the same market-wide gain. Interference changes the estimand.','grid')
op('sufficient','A B','Outcome','[(()=>{if(!p.every(x=>x===0||x===1))throw Error("Invalid binary cause");return Math.max(p[0],p[1]);})()]',lambda p:[max(p[0],p[1])],'Two toy binary causes A={A},B={B} each suffice for an outcome: Y=A OR B. What outcome is generated?','When both causes suffice, removing either alone may leave the outcome unchanged. A but-for test and the full structural causal account can differ.','grid')
OPS['sufficient']['binary']=True
op('preempt','TimeA TimeB','FirstTime Cause','[Math.min(p[0],p[1]),p[0]<p[1]?1:p[1]<p[0]?2:0]',lambda p:[min(p[0],p[1]),1 if p[0]<p[1] else 2 if p[1]<p[0] else 0],'Two toy sufficient events would arrive at times {TimeA},{TimeB}; the first ends the process. At what time does the outcome occur?','Timing can distinguish the event that actually occurs first from a backup that would have sufficed later. The structural stopping rule is part of the model.','grid')

def maxent(p):
 lo=-10;hi=10;n=int(p[0])
 for _ in range(100):
  t=(lo+hi)/2;w=[math.exp(t*i) for i in range(1,n+1)];mean=sum((i+1)*w[i] for i in range(n))/sum(w)
  if mean<p[1]:lo=t
  else:hi=t
 return [(lo+hi)/2]
op('maxent','Sides Mean','Tilt','[(()=>{let lo=-10,hi=10;for(let k=0;k<100;k++){const t=(lo+hi)/2,w=Array.from({length:p[0]},(_,i)=>Math.exp(t*(i+1))),mean=w.reduce((s,x,i)=>s+(i+1)*x,0)/w.reduce((a,b)=>a+b,0);if(mean<p[1])lo=t;else hi=t;}return (lo+hi)/2;})()]',maxent,'On toy faces 1 through {Sides}, use maximum-entropy probabilities proportional to exp(tilt×face), constrained to mean {Mean}. What tilt solves the mean by bisection on [-10,10]?','A maximum-entropy distribution depends on the stated support and moment constraint. Changing that information changes the resulting least-committal model.','grid')
op('overlap','TLow CLow THigh CHigh','Supported','[(p[0]>0&&p[1]>0?1:0)+(p[2]>0&&p[3]>0?1:0)]',lambda p:[int(p[0]>0 and p[1]>0)+int(p[2]>0 and p[3]>0)],'Two toy strata have treated/control counts {TLow}/{CLow} and {THigh}/{CHigh}. How many strata contain both treatment assignments?','An observed comparison needs support for both assignments in the relevant strata. Modeling unsupported strata adds extrapolation assumptions rather than creating observed overlap.','grid')
# Binary states in transfer questions must remain valid states.
OPS['logloss']['ts']='[(()=>{if(![0,1].includes(p[0])||p[1]<=0||p[1]>=1)throw Error("Invalid Bernoulli inputs");return -p[0]*Math.log(p[1])-(1-p[0])*Math.log(1-p[1]);})()]'
OPS['collider']['ts']='[(()=>{if(![0,1].includes(p[2])||![0,1].includes(p[3])||p[0]<=0||p[0]>=1||p[1]<=0||p[1]>=1)throw Error("Invalid selection inputs");return p[2]===1&&p[3]===0?1:p[1];})()]'
op('weightedcontrast','Weight EffectA EffectB','Contrast','[p[0]*p[1]+(1-p[0])*p[2]]',lambda p:[p[0]*p[1]+(1-p[0])*p[2]],'A toy estimator combines effects {EffectA},{EffectB} with weights {Weight} and 1-Weight, permitting negative weights. What contrast results?','A nonconvex comparison can fall outside the component-effect range. These are estimator weights, not population shares.','grid')
op('quadrature','VarA VarB','SD','[Math.sqrt(p[0]+p[1])]',lambda p:[math.sqrt(p[0]+p[1])],'Two independent zero-mean toy errors have variances {VarA},{VarB}. What is the standard deviation of their sum?','Independent variances add before taking a square root. Adding standard deviations would answer a different question; covariance would add another term.','grid')
